// @emit-error emitProgram: nested arrays are not supported
// A generic type ALIAS whose body puts the type PARAMETER under TWO arrays
// (`type Box<T> = { v: T[][] }`). `vl check` is rc=0; every instantiation is an emit
// error. The sibling `type-param-list-field.vl` (ONE array) runs, and so does the
// concrete twin `type Box = { v: i32[][] }` — nested arrays are supported, so the
// message names a construct that is not the problem.
//
// THE ANSWER IS NOT "NESTED ARRAYS", AND NOT THE RESULTING TYPE. The decisive probe
// is the neighbour `type-param-list-field-nested-arg.vl`: `type Box<T> = { v: T[] }`
// applied at `Box<i32[]>` produces the SAME `{v: i32[][]}` shape and RUNS. What
// separates them is the DEPTH the parameter sits at IN THE ALIAS BODY, nothing about
// the type that comes out.
//
// MECHANISM, read from the emitter's own tables (an instrumented build dumping the
// ref-list rows at the reject):
//
//   ROWS: [0] i32[] /kind 4   [1] T[] /kind 1
//   FLD  si=0 fi=0 elemName=T[] elemTyIx=-1 arenaLeg=-1 nameLeg=1
//
// The substituted shape's field `v` records its ref-list ELEMENT as `T[]` — the
// UN-substituted parameter. Row 0 is the row this literal needs (`i32[]`, element
// kind 4 = a nested i32 list) and it is already interned; nothing points at it.
// `emitObj`'s code-5 arm threads `sFieldRefSlot` = row 1, whose element kind is 1
// (a STRUCT element), so `emitArr`'s ref-list branch reaches the guard that forbids
// an array element and raises the message above.
//
// ANCHOR — `gaeApplyFieldTy` (`compiler/emit_base.vl`), whose whole body is:
//
//     if tn == pnames[i] { return args[i] }
//     if tn == pnames[i] + "[]" { return args[i] + "[]" }
//
// Two depths, and its own header says so ("a declared param name (or its `T[]` array
// form)"). `T[][]` matches neither, so it is returned unchanged. This is the THIRD
// appearance of the shape #1218 and #1230 each found — a classifier taught half a set
// its sibling has whole — and it is the SIBLING of the one #1230 fixed: #1230 taught
// `genTyParamArr` to peel to the LEAF (`arrLeafNameOf`) so the DECLARATION stopped
// drawing a wrong message; the APPLICATION's substitution kept its two-depth ladder.
//
// THE REMEDY IS MEASURED, NOT SKETCHED. Peeling `tn` to its leaf, matching the leaf
// against the parameter and re-attaching the same `[]` depth turns 8 cells green
// (`T[][]` at i32 / string / struct leaves, a second field beside it, `T[][][]`, an
// array-typed argument, and one of two parameters nested) and breaks 1: with no
// SHADOW GUARD it swallows `type Box<i32> = { v: i32[][] }` applied at `Box<string>`,
// which master lowers correctly today. That guard is #1230's own bycatch, and
// building it found a LIVE invalid-wasm hole on master at the depths that DO
// substitute — `type Box<i32> = { v: i32 }` + `const b: Box<string> = { v: 1 }` is
// `vl check`-clean and emits a module that does not parse. `gaeApplyFieldTy` has no
// shadow guard at any depth. Both are filed; `compiler/emit_base.vl` was outside this
// slice's partition.
//
// Refuted along the way: "nested arrays as such" (the concrete twin runs), "the
// element type" (i32 / string / struct leaves read the same cell), "the resulting
// type" (the same `{v: i32[][]}` runs when it is spelled `Box<i32[]>` over `T[]`),
// and "the declaration" (`type-param-list-field-uninstantiated.vl`'s axis — the
// declaration ALONE compiles here; only the APPLICATION rejects).
type Box<T> = { v: T[][] }

const b: Box<i32> = { v: [[1, 2]] }

print(b.v.length)
